---

<Layout
  title="Your package.json shows 20 dependencies. Your lock file has 487. — Commit"
  description="Full lock file support: scan all resolved transitive dependencies, not just your direct ones. The riskiest packages are frequently two hops in — invisible to package.json audits."
  canonical="https://getcommit.dev/blog/lockfile-scan/"
  publishDate="2026-04-28"
>

  <!-- Nav -->
  <nav>
    <div class="container nav-inner">
      <a href="/" class="nav-brand">Commit</a>
      <ul class="nav-links">
        <li><a href="/extension">Extension</a></li>
        <li><a href="/blog">Writing</a></li>
        <li><a href="/pricing">Pricing</a></li><li><a href="/docs">Docs</a></li><li><a href="https://github.com/piiiico/proof-of-commitment" target="_blank" rel="noopener">GitHub</a></li>
        <li><a href="mailto:pico@amdal.dev">Contact</a></li>
      </ul>
    </div>
  </nav>

  <!-- Essay -->
  <article class="essay">
    <div class="container">
      <div class="editorial">

        <!-- Header -->
        <header class="essay-header">
          <div class="essay-meta animate-in delay-1">
            <time datetime="2026-04-28">April 28, 2026</time>
          </div>
          <h1 class="essay-title animate-in delay-2">Your package.json shows 20 dependencies. Your lock file has 487.</h1>
          <p class="essay-subtitle animate-in delay-3">
            Full lock file support for supply chain risk scanning. Audit all resolved transitive
            dependencies &mdash; not just the ones you added directly.
          </p>
          <p class="essay-byline animate-in delay-4">H&aring;kon &Aring;mdal</p>
        </header>

        <!-- Body -->
        <div class="essay-body">

          <p>
            When you run <code>npm audit</code>, it checks your direct dependencies against a CVE database.
            When ua-parser-js was compromised in October 2021, <code>npm audit</code> showed zero issues. The
            attack vector was already visible in public data &mdash; a sole maintainer with millions of weekly
            downloads &mdash; but there was no CVE yet to match against.
          </p>

          <p>
            I built a tool that scores packages on behavioral signals instead of CVE databases. It&rsquo;s been
            useful for auditing direct dependencies. Today I shipped something I&rsquo;ve wanted for a while:
            <strong>full lock file support</strong>.
          </p>

          <pre><code># Before: audits direct deps only (package.json)
npx proof-of-commitment --file package.json

# Now: audits ALL resolved dependencies (lock file)
npx proof-of-commitment --file package-lock.json
npx proof-of-commitment --file yarn.lock
npx proof-of-commitment --file pnpm-lock.yaml</code></pre>

          <h2>What&rsquo;s different</h2>

          <p>
            Your <code>package.json</code> might have 15&ndash;20 direct dependencies. Your
            <code>package-lock.json</code> has the full resolved tree &mdash; often 300&ndash;500 packages.
            The risky packages are frequently <em>not</em> in your direct dependencies. They&rsquo;re two hops in.
          </p>

          <p>
            This is what I found when I audited <code>@anthropic-ai/sdk</code> via lock file:
          </p>

          <ul>
            <li>The SDK itself scores fine &mdash; 14 maintainers, good release history</li>
            <li>But <code>json-schema-to-ts</code> &mdash; a transitive dep &mdash; has 1 maintainer and 12M weekly downloads</li>
            <li>And <code>ts-algebra</code> &mdash; another transitive dep &mdash; has 1 maintainer and hasn&rsquo;t released in 12+ months</li>
          </ul>

          <p>
            Neither appears in a direct <code>package.json</code> audit. Both show up immediately with lock file scanning.
          </p>

          <h2>How it works</h2>

          <p>The CLI now:</p>

          <ol>
            <li>Parses your lock file to extract all resolved package names</li>
            <li>Batches them into groups of 20 and scores all batches in parallel</li>
            <li>Sorts results by risk score (CRITICAL first, then HIGH, etc.)</li>
            <li>Shows the highest-risk packages with a summary: &ldquo;3 CRITICAL packages found in 487 scanned&rdquo;</li>
          </ol>

          <p>
            For a typical Next.js project, this means scanning 400+ packages in about 15 seconds.
            For a minimal Node.js service, maybe 80 packages in 5 seconds.
          </p>

          <h2>What CRITICAL means</h2>

          <p>
            CRITICAL = sole maintainer + &gt;10M weekly downloads. That&rsquo;s the structural risk profile
            shared by ua-parser-js before the October 2021 compromise. It&rsquo;s also the profile of
            chalk (413M/wk), minimatch (560M/wk), glob (332M/wk), esbuild (190M/wk) &mdash; packages
            you&rsquo;re almost certainly running in production right now, probably via a lock file dep
            you&rsquo;ve never looked at.
          </p>

          <h2>Try it</h2>

          <p>Zero install, any Node.js project:</p>

          <pre><code>npx proof-of-commitment --file package-lock.json</code></pre>

          <p>
            Or paste your packages in the browser at <a href="/audit">getcommit.dev/audit</a>.
            The tool is open source at <a href="https://github.com/piiiico/proof-of-commitment" target="_blank" rel="noopener">github.com/piiiico/proof-of-commitment</a>.
          </p>

          <p style="margin-top: 2rem; padding-top: 1.5rem; border-top: 1px solid var(--border-light); font-size: 0.875rem; color: var(--ink-muted);">
            If you want this in your AI assistant: the MCP server at
            <code>poc-backend.amdal-dev.workers.dev/mcp</code> works with Claude Desktop,
            Cursor, and any MCP-compatible tool. Ask: &ldquo;Audit the dependencies in vercel/ai&rdquo;
            and it fetches the repo, scores everything, returns a risk table.
          </p>

        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </article>

</Layout>
